    {
      "id": "allowance-for-error/p1",
      "type": "text",
      "variant": "body",
      "content": "Until the end of the eighteenth century, a musket was a single object in many pieces. The lock, the stock and the barrel were filed and scraped against one another until they worked together, and they worked only with each other. A broken part could not be replaced from a box. It had to be made again, by hand, to suit the particular gun it belonged to. The craftsman who did this was called a fitter, and every machine needed one.",
      "order": 13,
      "relates_to": [],
      "spread_id": "s-2",
      "x": null,
      "y": null,
      "w": 79,
      "h": 55,
      "z": 4,
      "tone": "dark",
      "stack_id": "stack-2",
      "stack_index": 0
    },
    {
      "id": "allowance-for-error/p2",
      "type": "text",
      "variant": "body",
      "content": "In 1785 a French gunsmith named Honoré Blanc invited visitors to his workshop and laid out the parts of fifty musket locks, sorted into bins. Thomas Jefferson, then the American minister in Paris, assembled several locks himself from pieces taken at random. They all worked. What he had seen was not better craftsmanship. It was the absence of the fitter.",
      "order": 14,
      "relates_to": [],
      "spread_id": "s-2",
      "x": null,
      "y": null,
      "w": 79,
      "h": 45,
      "z": 5,
      "tone": "dark",
      "stack_id": "stack-2",
      "stack_index": 1
    },
    {
      "id": "allowance-for-error/img-lock",
      "type": "image",
      "variant": null,
      "content": null,
      "order": 15,
      "relates_to": [
        "allowance-for-error/p1",
        "allowance-for-error/p2"
      ],
      "spread_id": "s-2",
