      "z": 3,
      "tone": "dark"
    },
    {
      "id": "allowance-for-error/p3",
      "type": "text",
      "variant": "body",
      "content": "Blanc’s method was to stop comparing parts with each other and compare them with something else: a set of hardened steel gauges. A part was finished when it passed the gauge, not when it suited its neighbour. This sounds like a small change. It moved the idea of “correct” out of the finished gun and into an object that sat in a drawer.",
      "order": 17,
      "relates_to": [],
      "spread_id": "s-2",
      "x": null,
      "y": null,
      "w": 79,
      "h": 40,
      "z": 6,
      "tone": "dark",
      "stack_id": "stack-2",
      "stack_index": 2
    },
    {
      "id": "allowance-for-error/sub-limit",
      "type": "heading",
      "variant": "subhead",
      "content": "THE LIMIT IS THE DESIGN",
      "order": 18,
      "relates_to": [],
      "spread_id": "s-2",
      "x": null,
      "y": null,
      "w": 79,
      "h": 5,
      "z": 7,
      "tone": "dark",
      "stack_id": "stack-2",
      "stack_index": 3
    },
    {
      "id": "allowance-for-error/p4",
      "type": "text",
      "variant": "body",
      "content": "A gauge cannot ask for perfection, because no part is ever exactly the size it is meant to be. So it asks for something achievable: not smaller than this, not larger than that. The gap between the two limits is the tolerance. It is a written statement of how wrong a part is allowed to be and still count as right.",
      "order": 19,
      "relates_to": [],
      "spread_id": "s-2",
      "x": null,
      "y": null,
      "w": 79,
      "h": 35,
      "z": 8,
      "tone": "dark",
      "stack_id": "stack-2",
      "stack_index": 4
    },
    {
      "id": "allowance-for-error/p5",
      "type": "text",
      "variant": "body",
      "content": "Tolerances cost money. Halving the permitted error usually far more than doubles the work, since it demands better machines, slower cuts and more rejected parts. A good designer therefore does the opposite of what precision seems to mean, and makes every tolerance as loose as the job will bear. Errors also add up. Ten parts in a row, each within its limits, can together miss by ten times as much, so error has to be budgeted across a design the way money is.",
      "order": 20,
      "relates_to": [],
      "spread_id": "s-2",
      "x": null,
      "y": null,
      "w": 79,
      "h": 55,
      "z": 9,
      "tone": "dark",
      "stack_id": "stack-3",
      "stack_index": 0
    },
    {
      "id": "allowance-for-error/sub-strangers",
      "type": "heading",
      "variant": "subhead",
      "content": "STRANGERS WHO NEVER MEET",
      "order": 21,
      "relates_to": [],
      "spread_id": "s-2",
      "x": null,
      "y": null,
      "w": 79,
      "h": 5,
      "z": 10,
      "tone": "dark",
      "stack_id": "stack-3",
      "stack_index": 1
    },
    {
      "id": "allowance-for-error/p6",
      "type": "text",
      "variant": "body",
      "content": "The idea travelled slowly. Eli Whitney promised the United States interchangeable muskets in 1798 and never truly delivered them.^1^ It took until the 1820s, at the Harpers Ferry armory, for John Hall to build rifles whose parts really could be swapped, and it took him a workshop full of special machines and gauges to do it.",
      "order": 22,
      "relates_to": [],
      "spread_id": "s-2",
      "x": null,
      "y": null,
      "w": 79,
      "h": 40,
      "z": 11,
      "tone": "dark",
      "stack_id": "stack-3",
      "stack_index": 2
    },
