    {
      "id": "allowance-for-error/p7",
      "type": "text",
      "variant": "body",
      "content": "The result is so ordinary now that it is hard to see. A bolt made in one country fits a nut made in another by people who will never speak. The agreement that once took place between two hands at a bench happens in advance, on paper, between strangers. The same move appears wherever separately made things must work together: timetables, contracts, software interfaces. It holds only where fit can be checked by something like a gauge. Where it cannot, we are back to fitting.",
      "order": 25,
      "relates_to": [],
      "spread_id": "s-2",
      "x": null,
      "y": null,
      "w": 79,
      "h": 60,
      "z": 12,
      "tone": "dark",
      "stack_id": "stack-3",
      "stack_index": 3
    },
    {
      "id": "allowance-for-error/fn1",
      "type": "note",
      "variant": null,
      "content": "Whitney’s celebrated demonstration of 1801 is now generally thought to have used specially prepared parts. See Robert S. Woodbury, “The Legend of Eli Whitney and Interchangeable Parts”, *Technology and Culture*, 1960.",
