    {
      "id": "melencolia/t1",
      "type": "text",
      "variant": "body",
      "content": "A winged figure sits among the instruments of every exact art: compass, scale, plane, saw, hourglass. She is holding the compass and not using it. Nothing in the picture is broken. Everything needed for work is within reach, and no work is being done.",
      "order": 32,
      "relates_to": [],
      "spread_id": "s-3",
      "x": null,
      "y": null,
      "w": 86,
      "h": 30,
      "z": 5,
      "tone": "dark",
      "stack_id": "stack-6",
      "stack_index": 0
    },
    {
      "id": "melencolia/t2",
      "type": "text",
      "variant": "body",
      "content": "The best known reading, Erwin Panofsky’s, takes the print as a portrait of the mind that can measure everything and still cannot reach what it wants to know. Other scholars disagree, and Dürer left no explanation.",
      "order": 33,
      "relates_to": [],
      "spread_id": "s-3",
      "x": null,
      "y": null,
      "w": 86,
      "h": 25,
